# Supabase Best Practices

## Project Boundaries

- Keep Supabase integrations in focused server-side services. Follow the established Next.js App Router and authentication design; do not introduce Supabase Auth or a client state library unless the feature requires it.
- Prisma migrations own the application's tables, constraints, and RLS policies. Keep platform-specific setup, such as private Storage bucket creation, in documented Supabase SQL setup scripts.
- Check current Supabase documentation and changelog before implementing or changing a Supabase feature, CLI workflow, or security configuration.

## Security and Data Access

- Enable RLS on tables in exposed schemas and write policies for the actual user/resource ownership model. Authentication alone is not authorization; do not use blanket access policies.
- Never use user-editable `user_metadata` for authorization. Keep service-role or secret keys in server-only code; browser code may use only publishable keys.
- Validate and authorize every request on the server. Use parameterized queries; never build SQL by interpolating untrusted input.
- Keep private files in private buckets. Check the requesting user's right to the resource before returning a short-lived signed URL or handling a download.
- Handle Supabase response errors explicitly and return user-safe messages. Log useful server-side context without secrets or sensitive user data.
- Use Realtime only for features that require live updates. Keep subscriptions bounded and clean them up when their owner is disposed.

## Workflow and Verification

- Make schema changes through versioned migrations, review the SQL, and test against local Postgres before deployment. Do not make untracked production Dashboard edits.
- Keep local, staging, and production environments separate. Store credentials outside version control and limit production access.
- Test authorization boundaries, RLS behavior, and Storage access with the local database or Supabase test project. Use manual checks against deployed Supabase for the end-to-end flow.
- Monitor query plans and add indexes for measured access patterns. Use bounded queries and pagination for large result sets; configure backups and review platform health and security advisories.
